From: Jared Hooker <jaredroberthooker@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: iwlwifi: stop peripheral polling when NIC access fails

After an AX211 loses power across s2idle, iwl_pcie_rx_stop() polls for RX
DMA idle while the NIC is inaccessible. iwl_read_prph() converts a failed
NIC-access attempt into 0x5a5a5a5a, so iwl_poll_prph_bit() repeats the
expensive access timeout rather than recognizing that no register was read.

A function-graph trace on a ThinkPad L14 Gen 3 running linux-omarchy
7.2.8-9 measured 7.91 seconds in iwl_poll_prph_bit(), despite the caller's
1000-microsecond timeout. The surrounding PCI resume took 8.26 seconds.

Acquire NIC access explicitly for each read and return -ETIMEDOUT on
failure. Keep the existing acquire/read/release sequence and polling
interval for accessible hardware; do not reset or unload the driver.

Signed-off-by: Jared Hooker <jaredroberthooker@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/intel/iwlwifi/iwl-io.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/intel/iwlwifi/iwl-io.c
@@ -158,7 +158,16 @@
 	int t = 0;
 
 	do {
-		if ((iwl_read_prph(trans, addr) & mask) == (bits & mask))
+		u32 val;
+
+		/* Do not retry register reads when the NIC is inaccessible. */
+		if (!iwl_trans_grab_nic_access(trans))
+			return -ETIMEDOUT;
+
+		val = iwl_read_prph_no_grab(trans, addr);
+		iwl_trans_release_nic_access(trans);
+
+		if ((val & mask) == (bits & mask))
 			return 0;
 		udelay(IWL_POLL_INTERVAL);
 		t += IWL_POLL_INTERVAL;
